# Every text file is LF in the index and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` says locally.
#
# This used to read `* text=lf`, which does nothing at all: `text` is a boolean
# (or `auto`), so git reads any other value as "unspecified" and falls back to
# `core.autocrlf` -- which Git for Windows installs as `true`. So Windows
# checkouts got CRLF, and a test that writes a fixture from a `.feature`
# docstring and then asserts a line number in the output got its lines counted
# twice: behave keeps the CR on each docstring line, the fixture step writes it
# through a text-mode handle (CR LF -> CR CR LF), and reading that back with
# universal newlines turns one line break into two. That is what put the four
# `features/lint.feature` ASSY findings a line late on windows-latest, and only
# there.
#
# `text=auto` rather than a bare `text`, because `*` covers the binaries too: a
# bare `text` declares every one of them to be text and rewrites its bytes,
# while `auto` sniffs the content first. The sniff is a safety net, though, not
# the rule -- every binary kind in the tree is named below, so what happens to
# it is declared rather than guessed.
#
# `.pdf` earns its line the hard way. The sniff calls a blob binary on a NUL
# byte, or on nonprintable bytes outweighing printable ones;
# `examples/feature_render_custom/bracket.pdf` has no NUL at all and four high
# bytes in 60KB, so `auto` calls it text. It survives today only because it
# happens to contain no CR. That file is generated (`pc render`), and the next
# drawing that puts a CR inside a stream loses a byte on checkout, silently --
# measured: re-render with one CRLF in it and `auto` alone hands back 60710
# bytes where 60711 went in. `.3mf` is a zip container and the sniff does catch
# it; named here too, so that neither rests on the heuristic.
#
# `.ico` is one file, `ide/standalone/resources/partcad-ide.ico`, the
# Windows IDE's icon, and `.bmp` is the pair beside it that Inno Setup draws the
# installer's wizard with. They are deliberately not LFS like the `.png` and
# `.jpg` below: they are build *inputs* -- `build.sh` reads them and Inno Setup
# compiles them into the installer -- so a checkout that has not fetched LFS
# would hand the build a pointer file and ship an installer wearing a text file
# for an icon, silently. Most of the images LFS carries are outputs that nothing
# reads back.
#
# `logo_128x128.png` is the exception among the `.png`s, and it is here because
# the rule above was learned the hard way a second time: it is the VS Code
# extension's icon, `vsce package` copies it into the `.vsix`, and no checkout
# in `.github/workflows` asks for LFS -- so every published extension until now
# carried a 130-byte pointer file where its icon should be, and showed none in
# the marketplace or in the Extensions view. Named on its own rather than by
# extension: the rest of the `.png`s in the tree really are rendered output.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.step binary
*.stl binary
*.3mf binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
*.bmp binary
**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
/ide/vscode/resources/logo_128x128.png -filter binary

# The dev container's polyglot initializeCommand shim: cmd.exe reads the top of it
# and /bin/sh the bottom, and the shell half breaks on a trailing CR -- so pin it to
# LF whatever the checkout's core.eol says.
/.devcontainer/docker-socket-init.cmd text eol=lf
